WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.711
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the
program today, Congressman Roe Connor, who

00:00:03.711 --> 00:00:09.170
represents California's 17th District, located
in the heart of Silicon Valley.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.290
Congressman, always great to speak with you.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:13.250
Speaker 5: Always a pleasure, David.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.360
Speaker 1: So there's so many things to talk
about.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:22.060
Let's start with the United States Innovation
and Competition Act of twenty twenty one,

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.759
formally known as the Endless Frontier Act.

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:28.140
What what's the goal here?

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.400
What would this legislation do?

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.469
Speaker 5: First of all, a big dramatic increase
in science and technology funding of the kind

00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:39.739
we haven't seen since the Apollo years, we're
at about point seven percent of GDP in science

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:40.739
research.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:44.570
Comparatively, China is almost double one
point three percent of their GDP.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.789
This would add two hundred and fifty billion,
over 100 hundred billion of that in science

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:51.010
and technology development.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:54.190
And it would put this across the country.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:59.129
So focusing on black communities, brown communities,
rural communities, making sure innovation

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:01.440
is accessible to places left behind.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:09.570
Speaker 1: When we look at something like
that, Bill, and we look at the infrastructure

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:15.120
proposal and we look at all of these different
big pieces of legislation that that could

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.979
be tackled and we run into the inevitable
questions about, OK, in the Senate, does it

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:23.659
need 60 votes?

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:26.509
And if not, can it be done with 50?

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:31.090
But if it can be done with the 50, do Democrats
have 50 or do they have forty nine or forty

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:32.090
eight?

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.329
Is this just going to be the question around
every piece of legislation for the foreseeable

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:37.329
future?

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:38.329
Speaker 5: It is.

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:41.370
And look, unfortunately, we're living in a
Senate which is 50.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:42.550
We thought we would do better.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:46.799
We didn't we need to do build on that majority
and really living in a house that only has

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:48.410
a five vote majority.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:53.409
So it is the challenge of governing with slim
majorities and it's the challenge of governing

00:01:53.409 --> 00:02:03.500
with a dysfunctional Senate which has a filibuster
rule that allows the minority and a legitimizes

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.500
obstruction.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.649
Speaker 1: I don't think you and I have talked
about this.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:11.760
I mean, in terms of the filibuster, there
are some who say just eliminate it, period,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.090
eliminate it altogether, and then those who
say, but that's also a double edged sword.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:21.621
There's these intermediate ideas of, well,
instead of saying you need you need 60 to

00:02:21.621 --> 00:02:28.590
end debate, make it that you need 40 actual
people to continue pushing it forward and

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:33.650
kind of like flip the onus around or require
it to be a talking filibuster.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.220
What's your view about this issue?

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.480
Speaker 5: My view is that we just need to
end the filibuster ended altogether and use

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.900
over and over again to suppress and obstruct
civil rights legislation, racial equality

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:46.900
legislation.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.700
In fact, that's been its primary use.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:54.480
And it was an invention of Calhoon and wanted
to give slave states the power to block progressive

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:55.650
legislation.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.390
But I'm for anything that will advance the
ball.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:02.269
So we're going to say, OK, we want to have
voting rights be an exception to the filibuster.

00:03:02.269 --> 00:03:03.269
I'm for that.

00:03:03.269 --> 00:03:08.970
If we're going to put the onus on senators
to actually speak and show up and it's going

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.610
to make it much harder to filibuster, I'm
for that.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:15.870
But ultimately, we need to do away with this
is not part of our Constitution.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.099
It's not part of our founders vision.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:21.239
Speaker 1: Do you worry about the argument
that is it's a double edged sword and it could

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:25.110
really come back and hurt Democrats when the
political circumstances in the division are

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.569
just slightly different?

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:28.549
Speaker 5: For two reasons I don't.

00:03:28.549 --> 00:03:31.540
First, the Republicans push their agenda through
anyway.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.640
That's how they got the tax cut.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:39.040
So a lot of the Republican agenda, let's cut
taxes, but slash government isn't subject

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.110
to the filibuster.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.560
It's a lot of our policies that are subject
to the filibuster.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:49.040
Second, in a country, I think our problem
is that our politics is too slow.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.800
Our change is too hard.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:58.120
I worry less between a zig zagging from one
place to another than I worry about institutional

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:01.880
roadblocks and gridlock and our inability
to do things.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:08.400
Speaker 1: When you think about any of the
bills that you're currently supporting and

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.230
that includes USACA and all of these other
things that we're talking about, infrastructure,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:20.330
etc., we had this ProPublica story a couple
of weeks ago where they identified that relative

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:24.850
to the growth of net worth, which, as I've
explained to my audience, that's not how we

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.330
tax in the United States.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:32.420
We tax unrealized gains, but it exposed that
there are if you're making money from assets

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:39.770
rather than labor, you can until you realize
those gains have significant tax free growth

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.850
of of one's net worth.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:48.380
Do you think that in the context of any bill
that's designed to help workers, that we also

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.490
need to be revisiting how we tax assets versus
labor?

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:55.230
Speaker 5: Yes, we need a wealth tax in this
country.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:56.230
I mean, think about this.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:05:02.640
In my district has 10 trillion dollars of
market value in the Bay Area companies.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.770
It's grown 35 percent in the Bay Area.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:06.440
Ten trillion dollars.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.910
We ought to be taxing some of that.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:10.540
And a lot of those billionaires, frankly,
would pay the tax.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:11.770
They won't oppose it.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:17.070
Instead, this bipartisan bill for infrastructure
is going to fund it by raising the gas tax

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.420
on to the index of inflation.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:23.540
So we're going to tax the working class who
basically have to drive 40 minutes because

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:27.320
they can't afford an apartment in San Francisco
or in New York.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:29.000
So they are commuting as is.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.090
And we're going to put the burden on them
to pay for infrastructure instead of on the

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:34.090
tech billionaires.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.270
And the multibillionaires produced all this
wealth.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.270
It makes no sense.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:40.530
Speaker 1: So I don't know about you.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:48.940
I see an actual tax on wealth as so unlikely
to pass in the next four years that I wonder

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.550
how much it's even worth talking about it.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:57.970
But some of the Biden proposals on either
capital gains or, you know, there's other

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.660
ideas that maybe are more politically feasible.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.630
How important do you think it is as someone
who's been advocating for the wealth tax and

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:08.100
other things that, quite frankly, I think
are unlikely to pass anytime soon?

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.880
How much is the balance in your mind between
trying to get something done that can actually

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.770
be done versus talking about what you see
as the gold standard?

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:21.590
Speaker 5: I think we get do both them and
I mean, Tom Suozzi was a moderate on Ways

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:25.950
and Means and Brendan Boyle, I've actually
come out now for a while, tax at one time.

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.150
Well, tax, not like more than two percent
every year.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:33.370
Yeah, that let's call it the Patriot tax with
the pandemic, all this wealth that was generated.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:34.660
Let's tax at one time.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:38.940
So that is because of people like you and
Elizabeth Warren and others who have put the

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:40.930
idea into the public discourse.

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:45.370
And what often happens is it takes years and
then finally something happens.

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:47.220
So we need to continue to do that.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.670
But we can't let the perfect be the enemy
of the good.

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:55.250
And if we can get some solid reforms now,
such as a step up in basis means if you buy

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:59.150
a million dollars of Facebook stock, you hold
it till the end of your life.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:03.370
If you want to have kids and you give it to
your kids, they should have to pay tax on

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:04.370
the capital gains.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:09.620
Right now, they don't have to literally you
can die with capital gains never having been

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.550
taxed and pass it on to your heirs.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.500
Biden closes that gap and it raises billions
of dollars.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:19.280
We ought to be increasing the tax on corporations,
so let's get those done.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:23.890
But ultimately, if you really want to fund
things in this country, we need a Wall Street

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:24.900
financial tax.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:25.980
We need a wealth tax.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:30.890
And I don't think that will hurt innovation
or economic growth, but it would fund the

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:35.070
basic infrastructure based on the people who
have made fortunes, inconceivable fortunes.

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:37.400
Speaker 1: Yeah, there's some pretty slick
stuff.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:41.280
And I don't know if by slick I even mean this
sarcastically or not.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.740
I mean, it's interesting to me that this can
be done.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:49.790
One can even to build on the idea of the stepped
up basis, one can even borrow against their

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:55.230
portfolio and in a sense, de facto realize
the gains, like you can actually get access

00:07:55.230 --> 00:08:01.410
to that growth without even having to pay
the capital gains and pass it on tax free.

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:04.950
I mean, that's quite a mechanism that exists
in the United States tax code.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.120
Speaker 5: It is a dividend.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:12.160
And trust me that it's all being used by these
billionaires or hiring lawyers.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.550
And, you know, in one sense, you can't blame
them.

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:19.530
I mean, you're they're taking advantage of
rules that Congress has written.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:23.110
So, yeah, it could be more patriotic, perhaps,
to pay tax.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:26.490
But how many people are going to say, yeah,
I want to pay more tax than I owe?

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:27.490
Right.

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:31.670
So what we really need to do is change the
rules, make sure that these billionaires who

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:35.181
produce all this wealth are being taxed fairly
on it.

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:39.370
And I don't think a billionaire sitting there
is going to think, OK, maybe I shouldn't,

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:40.940
or an aspiring billionaire.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:45.530
I'm not going to start the next Google because
the government may take two percent of my

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:46.530
net worth.

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:47.960
That's just not how entrepreneurs think.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:53.460
Speaker 1: How do you see the sort of tiering
of priorities when it comes to the financial

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:54.460
stuff?

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:58.380
And what I mean by that is sometimes I talk
to people who are on the left who I think

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:03.380
feel pressure to say basically once you're
making a couple hundred thousand dollars a

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:08.331
year or even five hundred thousand dollars
a year, that and making 10 million and one

00:09:08.331 --> 00:09:12.840
hundred million and a billion, it should really
be seen in the same the same way you're just

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.180
making a ton of money.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:19.160
And when I actually get into it with a lot
of people, we sort of get to realize that,

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:23.580
you know, there's a big difference between
making three hundred and fifty K with a couple

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:29.330
of kids in a big city versus people who are
making five fifty and five hundred million

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:30.330
dollars a year.

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:35.930
How important do you think it is to approach
those groups differently versus just wrap

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:38.410
everybody together
Speaker 5: is very important.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:41.880
Approach something differently because of
the real taxes have to be on the ultra rich

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:44.850
and the big corporations, and that's where
the real money is.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:50.220
And, you know, tax the people who are making
billions of dollars, some of it perhaps legitimately

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.110
look, they may have come up with a great idea.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:53.510
They see the digital revolution.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:54.510
Sure.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:55.800
You understand it's transforming an industry.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:59.090
They're at the forefront of leading that great
celebrated.

00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:03.200
But then they have to pay a tax so that society
can transition.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.170
But that's where the real taxes don't go after.

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:11.440
The person who's making a few hundred thousand
already has a couple of kids in college in

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:12.440
the suburbs.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:15.460
I mean, look, make sure it goes up to thirty
nine percent.

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:19.080
But that's not you don't want to stop the
the middle class or even the upper middle

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:23.280
class you want to do is say we've got we're
going to prioritize the working class, we're

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:26.910
going to look out for the middle class and
upper middle class, but we're really going

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:30.210
to get the revenue from the big corporations
and the ultra rich.

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:35.640
Speaker 1: And I think maybe this is something
that the truly ultra rich really benefit from,

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:41.450
which is that when they are sort of wrapped
in together with folks that are wealthy but

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:49.260
less wealthy, they are maybe better able to
take advantage of the tax law and end up paying

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:55.400
as an effective tax rate, even less like the
ultra rich often end up paying slightly less

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:59.420
than the very rich to distinguish between
two very rich groups.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:02.230
That maybe is something that would deserve
some significant attention.

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:03.230
Speaker 5: Absolutely.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.650
I think we have to treat the ultra rich as
a separate class, given the wealth generation

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:08.650
in this country.

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:12.280
And I, I think taxing them more, in my view,
is in their interests.

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.950
And this is why there's so many billionaires,
by the way, in my district who are fine with

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:16.960
the wealth tax.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:17.960
Yep.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:18.960
And.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:23.810
The reason it's in their interest is because
if you want to prevent a resentment and hostility

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:28.740
to wealth generation and a lot of their wealth
generation, in my view, has come through honest,

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:30.620
hard work, creativity and ingenuity.

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:33.130
It's not that they deserve all of the billions.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:34.840
There are lots of people that went into it.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:38.800
But it's not that they were just lucky they
didn't work hard.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:44.550
And but if you want people to celebrate that,
then pay the tax and show that you're generating

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.250
that wealth is helping society.

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.180
When you don't pay that tax, that's where
you have the resentment.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:56.130
Speaker 1: Last thing I wanted to ask you
about in terms of budget reconciliation, where

00:11:56.130 --> 00:12:01.940
would you like to see it used in order to
get one more big accomplishment in twenty

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:02.940
twenty one?

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:06.721
Speaker 5: Climate, clearly, it has to have
climate in it.

00:12:06.721 --> 00:12:11.180
I mean, the infrastructure plan that currently
is on the table is basically a version of

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:12.380
what Trump said.

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:13.690
It has none of the climate goals.

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:16.050
So we need the climate goals in there.

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:23.210
We need a broader vision on tax policy so
that you don't have the working class with

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:24.460
the tax policy.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:26.730
That would be my my essential part.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:28.500
I mean, obviously, I want to expand Medicare.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:32.990
I want prescription drugs out of a hole, a
child care, a whole vision.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:36.801
But if it's the White House were to call me
and said, what are the must haves, I take

00:12:36.801 --> 00:12:38.230
good tax policy and climate.

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:44.370
Speaker 1: Do you agree that for Democrats
to make a solid case for 20, 22, which really

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:49.680
is going to kick off I mean, it might be January,
it really might even be November, December.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:51.740
Some consultants are saying.

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:56.370
Do you think that Joe Biden needs one more
major accomplishment in addition to vaccine

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:59.540
rollout and covid stimulus this calendar year?

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:01.800
Speaker 5: I do, and I think he'll get one.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.840
But the question is, what kind will it be
if it is just a bipartisan infrastructure

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:09.220
bill that may help and it may help a moderate
swing districts.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:13.311
But I don't think it's going to mobilize their
base that we need both.e need something to

00:13:13.311 --> 00:13:17.930
the independents, swing voters, but we also
need to show our base that we really delivered

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:19.220
on progressive change.

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:25.090
So I would say he needs one big thing that
appeals to the base as well.

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:30.670
Speaker 1: Yeah, a relatively small infrastructure
bill that Mitt Romney loves.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:34.660
I don't know if that's necessarily going to
count as the progressive change people are

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:35.690
people are waiting for.

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:36.850
Completely agree with you.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:42.040
We've been speaking with Congressman Rokan,
representing California's 17th District.

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:43.470
Always great having you on, David.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:44.720
Speaker 5: Always enjoy it.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:45.350
Thank you.

